Push button Projects Overview

This folder contains a collection of basic push button projects created to explore and understand the fundamentals of reading and processing button inputs using an ESP32.
Each project focuses on a different method of detecting button presses and responding to user input, helping build a strong foundation before moving on to more advanced
electronics, sensors, and interactive embedded systems projects.

These examples demonstrate concepts such as digital input, GPIO configuration, button state detection, pull-up and pull-down resistors, toggling outputs,
using buttons to control LEDs, detecting multiple button inputs, and creating interactive hardware systems. They also serve as reference implementations that can
be reused in future robotics, IoT, and embedded system projects.

-Projects Included-

Basic Push Button

* A basic project that detects whether a push button is pressed or released and turns an LED on or off correspondingly. 
  This introduces digital input and teaches how a microcontroller reads the state of a physical switch.

Switch Button

* Uses a switch-type push button to control an LED, turning it on or off according to the button’s state. 
  This project demonstrates how digital inputs can be used to control digital outputs.

Multiple Button Inputs

* A project that uses two push buttons to control two LEDs. Pressing either button individually lights up LED1, while pressing both buttons simultaneously lights up LED2.
  This introduces digital inputs and logic conditions, teaching how a microcontroller can read multiple physical switches and control outputs based on their combined states.

Button Press Counter

* Uses a push button to count the number of times it is pressed and displays the current count through an output.
  This introduces input counting and variables, teaching how a microcontroller can track repeated button presses and store changing values.

Short and Long Press

* Detects whether a push button is pressed briefly or held down for a longer period of time, triggering different outputs for each type of press.
  This introduces timing and input detection, teaching how a microcontroller can measure the duration of a button press and respond accordingly.

Combination Lock

* Detects specific combinations of multiple button presses and performs different actions depending on the combination. This introduces conditional
  logic and more complex user input handling.

Reaction Time Tester





Learning Objectives

By completing these projects, you will gain experience with:

Configuring GPIO pins for button inputs
Reading digital input states
Using pull-up and pull-down resistors
Detecting button presses and releases
Using conditional statements with hardware inputs
Tracking button states with variables
Controlling outputs using button inputs
Handling multiple input devices
Creating interactive hardware systems
Applying programming logic to physical controls

These projects provide a solid foundation for more advanced applications involving keypads, switches, sensors, control panels, robotics, IoT devices, and
interactive embedded systems.



